\[
\boxed{
\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\textbf{Coupling of opposite‑parity components by the }\Lambda\text{-doubling terms.}\\[4pt]
In a }^2\Pi_{1/2}\text{ state the electronic wavefunction contains two degenerate components }|\Lambda=+1,\Sigma=-\tfrac12,\Omega=\tfrac12\rangle\text{ and }|\Lambda=-1,\Sigma=+\tfrac12,\Omega=\tfrac12\rangle.  Under the reflection operation that defines the e/f parity, }|\Lambda\rangle\to|-\Lambda\rangle\text{, so these two components have opposite parity (e and f).  The effective operators 
\[
p\,\mathbf{J}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{S}\,(J^{2}-\Omega^{2})\quad\text{and}\quad
q\big[(J^{2}-\Omega^{2})^{2}-\Omega^{2}\big]
\]
contain the factor }J_{\perp}^{2}=J^{2}-\Omega^{2}\text{, which is odd under that reflection.  Consequently they possess non‑zero off‑diagonal matrix elements }\langle\Lambda=+1|H_{\Lambda\text{-doubling}}|\Lambda=-1\rangle\neq0\text{, mixing the e and f levels.  The resulting eigenstates are linear combinations of the two opposite‑parity components, and the energy splitting produced is the Λ‑doubling.}\\[6pt]
\textbf{Absence in a }^2\Sigma^{+}\text{ state.}\\[4pt]
For a }^2\Sigma^{+}\text{ state }\Lambda=0\text{, so there is no partner with }-\Lambda\text{.  The operators that change the sign of }\Lambda\text{ (i.e., }J_{\perp}^{2}\text{) therefore act trivially, and the effective constants }p\text{ and }q\text{ are identically zero.  Hence a }^2\Sigma^{+}\text{ state exhibits no Λ‑doubling.}
\end{minipage}
}
\]

