u\,\frac{{\rm d}C_{\rm MeOH}}{{\rm d}z}
&=D_{\rm ax}\,\frac{{\rm d}^{2}C_{\rm MeOH}}{{\rm d}z^{2}}
-\rho_{c}\Bigl(k_{1}\,C_{\rm MeOH}\,C_{\rm O_{2}}^{1/2}
      +k_{2}\,C_{\rm MeOH}\,C_{\rm O_{2}}\Bigr),\\[6pt]
u\,\frac{{\rm d}C_{\rm O_{2}}}{{\rm d}z}
&=D_{\rm ax}\,\frac{{\rm d}^{2}C_{\rm O_{2}}}{{\rm d}z^{2}}
-\rho_{c}\Bigl[\tfrac12\,k_{1}\,C_{\rm MeOH}\,C_{\rm O_{2}}^{1/2}
               +\tfrac32\,k_{2}\,C_{\rm MeOH}\,C_{\rm O_{2}}\Bigr]
-\gamma\,k_{m}\,\bigl(C_{\rm O_{2}}-C_{\rm O_{2}}^{\rm perm}\bigr)\; .
\end{aligned}}
\]

*Explanation.*  
The steady‑state axial‑dispersion balance for a component \(i\) in a packed‑bed tube is  

\[
\frac{{\rm d}}{{\rm d}z}\Big(uC_i-D_{\rm ax}\frac{{\rm d}C_i}{{\rm d}z}\Big)
= -\text{(reaction consumption per unit reactor volume)} 
   -\text{(permeation sink, only for O\(_2\))}.
\]

Using the rates per unit catalyst mass (or volume) and the catalyst density
\(\rho_c\) (mass of catalyst per reactor volume) gives the reaction terms
\(\rho_c r_1\) and \(\rho_c r_2\).  Methanol is consumed in both reactions,
so its net consumption rate is \(\rho_c(r_1+r_2)\).  Oxygen is consumed at
\(\tfrac12\rho_c r_1+\tfrac32\rho_c r_2\) and additionally removed by the
membrane at the volumetric rate \(\gamma J_{O_2}
= \gamma k_m\,(C_{O_2}-C_{O_2}^{\rm perm})\), where \(\gamma=2\pi R/A\).

The porosity \(\varepsilon\) appears only in the transient term
\(\varepsilon\partial C_i/\partial t\); at steady state this term vanishes,
so \(\varepsilon\) does not appear explicitly in the final steady‑state
equations.  The equations above are therefore the required governing
equations for \(C_{\rm MeOH}(z)\) and \(C_{O_2}(z)\).

