The second‑order rate constant for the acid‑catalyzed hydrolysis of an ester bond is given by transition‑state theory  

\[
k_{2}=k_{0}\,\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\rm hyd}}{RT}\Big)
\qquad(k_{0}=1\times10^{13}\ {\rm s^{-1}})
\]

Because the acid concentration is constant, the reaction is pseudo‑first‑order in polymer:

\[
\frac{d[\mathrm B]}{dt}=-k_{\rm obs}[\mathrm B],
\qquad 
k_{\rm obs}=k_{2}[\mathrm{H}^{+}]
      =k_{0}[\mathrm{H}^{+}]\,\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\rm hyd}}{RT}\Big)
\]

For a first‑order process the half‑life is  

\[
t_{1/2}=\frac{\ln 2}{k_{\rm obs}}
       =\frac{\ln 2}{k_{0}[\mathrm{H}^{+}]\,\exp\!\big(-\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\rm hyd}/RT\big)}
       =\frac{\ln 2\,\exp\!\big(\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\rm hyd}/RT\big)}
              {k_{0}[\mathrm{H}^{+}]}
\]

Hence, at 298 K  

\[
\boxed{\,t_{1/2}
      =\frac{\ln 2\,\exp\!\big(\Delta G^{\ddagger}_{\rm hyd}/RT\big)}
             {k_{0}\,[\mathrm{H}^{+}]}\,}
\]

