For a symmetric double‑well the tunnelling splitting can be written in the WKB/instanton (or harmonic‑instanton) approximation as  

\[
\Delta E(\mu)\;\approx\;A\,\mu^{-1/2}\,
\exp\!\Big[-\,\frac{2}{\hbar}\int_{q_{a}}^{q_{b}}
\sqrt{2\mu\,[V(q)-E_{0}]}\;dq\Big] .
\]

The integral contains the mass only under the square‑root, therefore we separate the mass‑independent part  

\[
S_{0}\;=\;\frac{2}{\hbar}\int_{q_{a}}^{q_{b}}
\sqrt{2\,[V(q)-E_{0}]}\;dq ,
\]

so that  

\[
\boxed{\;\Delta E(\mu)=A\,\mu^{-1/2}\,
\exp\!\big[-\,S_{0}\,\sqrt{\mu}\,\big]\;}
\tag{1}
\]

with \(A\) and \(S_{0}\) determined solely by the (unchanged) potential‑energy surface.  

Equation (1) gives the desired mass‑scaling law.  In particular, for replacement of the transferring proton (mass \(\mu_{H}\)) by deuterium (\(\mu_{D}=3672\) a.u.) one obtains  

\[
\boxed{\;
\frac{\Delta E_{D}}{\Delta E_{H}}
=\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{H}}{\mu_{D}}\Bigr)^{1/2}
\exp\!\big[-S_{0}\big(\sqrt{\mu_{D}}-\sqrt{\mu_{H}}\big)\big]\;}
\tag{2}
\]

or, equivalently,

\[
\boxed{\;
\Delta E_{D}= \Delta E_{H}\,
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{H}}{\mu_{D}}\Bigr)^{1/2}
\exp\!\big[-S_{0}\big(\sqrt{\mu_{D}}-\sqrt{\mu_{H}}\big)\big]\; } .
\]

Thus the tunnelling splitting scales as \(\mu^{-1/2}\) in the prefactor and exponentially with \(\sqrt{\mu}\) in the barrier‑penetration factor.  For the hydrogen‑to‑deuterium substitution the prefactor is reduced by \((\mu_{H}/\mu_{D})^{1/2}\approx 1/\sqrt{3672}\approx 0.016\), and the exponential term further suppresses the splitting because \(\sqrt{\mu_{D}}>\sqrt{\mu_{H}}\).

