**Derivation**

For the extraction reaction  

\[
\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}_{\;(aq)}+2\,\mathrm{NO_3^-}_{\;(aq)}
\;\rightleftharpoons\;
\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}_{\;(IL)},
\]

the thermodynamic equilibrium constant (dimensionless) is defined in terms of activities:

\[
K\;=\;\frac{a_{\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}}^{\;IL}}
           {a_{\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}}^{\;aq}\,
            \bigl(a_{\mathrm{NO_3^-}}^{\;aq}\bigr)^{2}} .
\tag{1}
\]

Activities are related to concentrations by the respective activity coefficients:

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}}^{\;IL}&=\gamma^{\;IL}\,[\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}]_{IL},\\[2pt]
a_{\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}}^{\;aq}&=\gamma_{\mathrm{UO_2}}^{\;aq}\,[\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}]_{aq},\\[2pt]
a_{\mathrm{NO_3^-}}^{\;aq}&=\gamma_{\mathrm{NO_3}}^{\;aq}\,[\mathrm{NO_3^-}]_{aq}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]

Inserting (2) into (1) gives

\[
\gamma^{\;IL}\,[\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}]_{IL}
=
K\,
\gamma_{\mathrm{UO_2}}^{\;aq}\,
\bigl(\gamma_{\mathrm{NO_3}}^{\;aq}\bigr)^{2}\,
[\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}]_{aq}\,
[\mathrm{NO_3^-}]_{aq}^{\,2}.
\tag{3}
\]

The **distribution coefficient** \(D\) is the ratio of the total uranium concentration in the IL to that remaining in the aqueous phase:

\[
D\;=\;\frac{[\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}]_{IL}}{[\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}]_{aq}} .
\tag{4}
\]

Dividing (3) by \([\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}]_{aq}\) and solving for \(D\) yields

\[
D
= \frac{\gamma_{\mathrm{UO_2}}^{\;aq}\,
      \bigl(\gamma_{\mathrm{NO_3}}^{\;aq}\bigr)^{2}}
      {\gamma^{\;IL}}\;
   K\;
   [\mathrm{NO_3^-}]_{aq}^{\,2}.
\tag{5}
\]

The equilibrium constant is related to the standard Gibbs‑free‑energy change of the overall extraction reaction:

\[
K = \exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}}{RT}\Big),
\qquad
\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}
= \Delta G_f^{\circ}\bigl(\mathrm{UO_2(NO_3)_2}_{IL}\bigr)
  -\Delta G_f^{\circ}\bigl(\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}_{aq}\bigr)
  -2\Delta G_f^{\circ}\bigl(\mathrm{NO_3^-}_{aq}\bigr).
\tag{6}
\]

Because the standard free energies of formation are the sum of the gas‑phase values and the solvation free energies, the gas‑phase contributions cancel and the reaction free energy reduces to the difference of solvation free energies:

\[
\boxed{
\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}
=
\Big[\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{IL}(\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}})
      +2\,\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{IL}(\mathrm{NO_3^-})\Big]
-
\Big[\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{aq}(\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}})
      +2\,\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{aq}(\mathrm{NO_3^-})\Big]
}.
\tag{7}
\]

Substituting (6) into (5) gives the final expression for the equilibrium distribution coefficient:

\[
\boxed{
D
=
\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm{UO_2}}^{\;aq}\,
      \bigl(\gamma_{\mathrm{NO_3}}^{\;aq}\bigr)^{2}}
      {\gamma^{\;IL}}\;
\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}}{RT}\Big)\,
\bigl[\mathrm{NO_3^-}\bigr]_{aq}^{\,2}
}.
\tag{8}
\]

If the mean ionic activity coefficient for the pair \(\mathrm{UO_2^{2+}}/\mathrm{NO_3^-}\) is used as an approximation for each ion (\(\gamma_{\mathrm{UO_2}}^{\;aq}\approx\gamma_{\mathrm{NO_3}}^{\;aq}\approx\gamma_{\pm}^{aq}\)), Eq. (8) simplifies to

\[
\boxed{
D
=
\frac{\bigl(\gamma_{\pm}^{aq}\bigr)^{3}}{\gamma^{\;IL}}\;
\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}}{RT}\Big)\,
\bigl[\mathrm{NO_3^-}\bigr]_{aq}^{\,2}
}.
\tag{9}
\]

Equation (9) explicitly shows how the standard Gibbs‑free‑energy change \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{rxn}}^{\circ}\) (obtained from the solvation free‑energy data) and the activity coefficients enter the equilibrium distribution coefficient \(D\).

