The hydrolysis of the N‑hydroxymethyl intermediate and its further oxidation both require the high‑valent Fe‑oxo species.  Writing the elementary rates:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(i) hydrolysis:}&\qquad 
v_{\mathrm{hyd}} = k_{\mathrm{hyd}}\, [\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}  \\[4pt]
\text{(ii) oxidation:}&\qquad 
v_{\mathrm{ox}}   = k_{\mathrm{ox}} \, [\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}
\end{aligned}
\]

The hydrolysis constant is assumed to increase linearly with the mole fraction of water, \(x_{\mathrm{H_2O}}\):

\[
k_{\mathrm{hyd}} = k_{h}^{\,0}\; x_{\mathrm{H_2O}}
\qquad\text{(where }k_{h}^{\,0}\text{ is the intrinsic hydrolysis constant per unit }x_{\mathrm{H_2O}}\text{).}
\]

The oxidation constant, \(k_{\mathrm{ox}}\), is taken as independent of water.

Hence

\[
\frac{v_{\mathrm{hyd}}}{v_{\mathrm{ox}}}
   = \frac{k_{\mathrm{hyd}}[\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}}
          {k_{\mathrm{ox}}[\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}}
   = \frac{k_{h}^{\,0}\,x_{\mathrm{H_2O}}\,[\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}}
          {k_{\mathrm{ox}}\,[\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}}
   = \frac{k_{h}^{\,0}}{k_{\mathrm{ox}}}\;x_{\mathrm{H_2O}} .
\]

Thus the ratio of the two competing rates (or equivalently the ratio of the corresponding rate constants) is

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\frac{k_{\text{hydrolysis}}}{k_{\text{oxidation}}}
   = \frac{k_{h}^{\,0}}{k_{\text{ox}}}\;x_{\mathrm{H_2O}}
   = \alpha\,x_{\mathrm{H_2O}} } ,
\]

where \(\alpha = k_{h}^{\,0}/k_{\text{ox}}\) is a constant characteristic of the catalyst and reaction conditions.  The steady‑state concentration of Fe⁵⁺=O cancels because both steps are first‑order in \([\mathrm{Fe}^{V}=O]_{\!ss}\).

